      description: "This session, Centring Afrocentric and Indigenous Perspectives in Child Welfare Practice with BIPOC Children, explores how systemic racism and colonial legacies continue to shape the overrepresentation of Black, Indigenous, and other racialized children in child welfare systems. The session introduces Afrocentric and Indigenous frameworks as alternative approaches that center culture, identity, and community in supporting children and families.Participants will examine how Eurocentric child welfare models often contribute to surveillance, bias, and inequitable outcomes, and will explore how Afrocentric principles such as Ubuntu, collective responsibility, and cultural grounding can support more just and healing-centered practice.",
      speakers: [
        { name: "Samantha Campbell", title: "Social Worker", org: "", bio: "I am a registered social worker serving as a facilitator at a Child and Youth Advocacy Center (CYAC). I have experience in the fields of sexual and domestic violence, as well as child welfare. My work focuses on supporting individuals and families as they navigate complex and often challenging circumstances, while also being mindful of the wider social and structural factors that influence their experienceI hold a diploma in Social Work from Keyano College and a Bachelor of Social Work from the University of Calgary. Currently, I am pursuing a Master of Social Work (MSW) at Wilfrid Laurier University, where I am further developing my critical understanding of child welfare practice, systemic inequities, and the effects of colonization. As part of my studies, I am completing my practicum with The Child Welfare Truth-Telling Collective.My approach to social work is grounded in Ubuntu, the understanding that “I am because we are.” This belief is not just theoretical for me; it reflect" }
      ]
