      tags: ["child maltreatment reporting","collaborative practices","child protection","child welfare practice"],
      description: "This solution-oriented strategy session tackles the issue of child psychological maltreatment. We invite professionals of all kinds to come together and share their experiences – challenges, failures, good practices, and lessons learned – to understand what each sector needs to create more supportive trajectories for psychologically maltreated children. We then invite individuals with relevant experience – survivors and advocates – to contribute their perspective. We aim to use this discussion as a prototype, integrating both perspectives into a practical framework for how lived experiences of child psychological maltreatment can contribute to the work of child welfare professionals, making visible the experiences of survivors and supporting continued collaboration across sectors.",
      speakers: [
        { name: "Lisa Marie Schaumburg", title: "PhD Researcher", org: "KU Leuven", bio: "Lisa Marie Schaumburg is a psychologist and PhD researcher who works at the intersection of child protection, social policy, and evidence-based advocacy. Her interests focus on promoting the well-being and protection of children affected by family violence, both in her academic work and in collaboration with several European non-profit organizations. She is affiliated with KU Leuven, where she is currently working on a 4-year project about child psychological maltreatment. Her research incorporates the perspectives of children, caregivers, professionals, and policymakers to complete a holistic approach to psychological maltreatment. Lisa Marie is especially interested in interdisciplinary and collaborative approaches that align research, policy, lived experience, and frontline practice, hoping to facilitate detection and reporting of psychological maltreatment in children and adolescents and contributing to enduring changes for these vulnerable populations." }
      ]
