      description: "This workshop examines family violence in South Africa through a decolonial lens, challenging colonial assumptions that African families ignore harm. Despite apartheid’s disruptions, Ubuntu, collective decision‑making, and traditional family systems continue to offer strong frameworks for accountability and relational repair. Drawing on Bourdieu’s concepts of habitus, symbolic violence, and social capital, the session explores how colonialism destabilised Black family life while Indigenous care structures endured. Using a Zulu case study and the proverbs, the workshop shows how harm affects the whole kinship network and requires redress. It highlights how Indigenous practices align with FGC to restore relationships and support culturally grounded responses to family violence.",
