        { name: "Mthoko Ngobese-Sampson", title: "Dr", org: "Kingston University London", bio: "Mthoko Ngobese‑Sampson is a Senior Lecturer in Social Work at Kingston University, London. Her teaching, research, and practice are grounded in culturally competent, community‑centred approaches to working with children and families, shaped by a commitment to challenging oppression in all its forms. As a Zulu woman from South Africa, her lived experience continues to inform her pedagogy, scholarship, and public engagement, enabling her to equip practitioners, students, and communities with tools to challenge structural inequalities and promote dignity, culture, and collective wellbeing. Her doctoral research examined Family Group Conferencing (FGC) as a culturally competent model of practice, exploring how the philosophy of Ubuntu can strengthen collaboration with children and families in safeguarding contexts. This work contributes to wider debates on decolonising social work and foregrounds Indigenous relational ontologies as essential to effective practice." },
        { name: "Joan Pennell", title: "Professor Emerita of Social Work", org: "North Carolina State University", bio: "Professor Emerita of Social Work at North Carolina State University and Founding Director of the Center for Family and Community Engagement. She has conducted research on restorative justice, family violence, child welfare, and fathering. Before her return to the United States, she co-founded the first shelter for abused women and their children in Newfoundland and co-facilitated support groups for abused women of European heritage in Newfoundland and Indigenous descent in Manitoba. In the province of Newfoundland and Labrador, she worked with three culturally diverse communities to demonstrate that family group decision-making is effective in situations of child maltreatment and domestic violence. Later, she found the same results among the White and African American families in North Carolina with a history of slavery. Her work has been guided by her Quaker beliefs in regenerating the goodness within everyone. She has authored over 200 publications" }
